<div class="page active home-page"><section class="hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">Development · Inequality · Public Policy</div><h1>Ashton Milton</h1><p class="hero-subtitle">Inequality, development governance and Asia</p><p>I write about inequality, development governance and public policy, with a focus on East and Southeast Asia. My work examines how political institutions shape economic change, how its gains reach households and where public protection falls short.</p><p>I study Politics and Sociology at Queen Mary University of London and completed a year abroad at Waseda University in Tokyo. My background also includes journalism, community research and educational governance.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href="/writing/">Writing</a><a class="button" href="/about/">About</a></div></div></section><section><h2>Research and Writing Interests</h2><div class="grid-3"><article class="interest-card"><h3>Inequality and Social Protection</h3><p>How earnings, redistribution and welfare provision shape household living standards, and who remains exposed to insecurity.</p></article><article class="interest-card"><h3>Asia and Development</h3><p>The political economy of development in East and Southeast Asia, with current research on China and Vietnam.</p></article><article class="interest-card"><h3>Political Institutions and Public Policy</h3><p>How political incentives, public finance and administrative capacity shape policy priorities and the delivery of public services.</p></article></div></section><section><div class="editorial-copy"><h2>Current Research</h2><h3>Rising Powers, Unequal Lives</h3><p>The Political Economy of Household Gains in China and Vietnam, 2010–2025</p><p class="label">Research in progress</p><p>My dissertation asks how earnings and redistribution have shaped household income gains in China and Vietnam. I will first examine whether their contributions differ between the countries, then consider how political incentives and public finance might explain the patterns.</p><a class="button" href="/research/">Research Outline</a></div></section><section><h2>Featured Writing</h2><div class="grid-3">
